<p>Suppose the home page of the shop shows a "trending products" panel. Building it takes one aggregate query that runs for about 200 milliseconds, so the result is cached in Redis under the key <code>trending</code> with <code>EX 60</code>, and every page view reads it from there. The site serves about 500 page views a second. The hit ratio for the key is about 99.7%, and yet once a minute the database's graph shows a spike and the home page is slow for a moment.</p>
<p>Nothing is broken. The spike is what cache-aside does when a key that many requests share expires: for a short time every one of them misses, and every one of them asks the database the same question. This is called a <strong>cache stampede</strong>. It is also called dog-piling, and often the thundering herd, a name borrowed from operating systems for many waiting processes woken at once to compete for one thing. (Facebook's memcache paper uses thundering herd in a narrower sense, for a key that is both read heavily and written often; that case gets its own section below.)</p>
<p>This article works out how large a stampede is and why it hurts the database more than its size suggests, then goes through the standard defences in order: coalescing requests inside a process, a rebuild lock in Redis, serving the old copy while one request refreshes it, and refreshing early at random. It ends with the cases where the trigger is something other than one key expiring: a write that deletes a hot key, many keys expiring together, and a cold start, where every key is missing at once. By the end you will be able to predict which keys can stampede, choose a defence for each, and implement it with the right Redis commands and their failure modes in mind.</p>

<section id="expiry">
<h2>What happens when a popular key expires</h2>
<p>Figure 1 slows the situation down so you can watch it. <Term kind="client" mark>Requests</Term> arrive from the left and are spread over four application processes. Each process reads the key from <Term kind="redis" mark>Redis</Term>. While the copy is there, the read is a <Term kind="hit" mark>hit</Term>. When the copy has reached its <Term kind="expiry" mark>expiry</Term>, the read is a <Term kind="miss" mark>miss</Term>, and the process asks the <Term kind="database" mark>database</Term>, then stores the answer back in Redis with a <Term kind="write" mark>write</Term>. Here the copy lives for 5 seconds and a rebuild, from the miss to the stored copy, takes 1 second.</p>
<StampedeFigure number={1} />
<p>Watch the database while the copy is fresh: nothing reaches it. Then the copy expires, and for one second every request misses. The first miss has sent a query, but until its answer is stored, Redis still has nothing, so the second request misses too, and the third. Each sends the same query and each eventually stores the same answer. The readout counts the queries per expiry. Drag the rate down to 5 requests a second and there are about 5; drag it up to 40 and there are about 40.</p>
</section>

<section id="size">
<h2>How big a stampede is</h2>
<p>The count in Figure 1 follows from two numbers. Say requests for the key arrive at <var>r</var> a second and a rebuild takes <var>δ</var> seconds from the first miss until the new copy is stored. The first miss starts the rebuild. Every request that arrives during the next <var>δ</var> seconds finds the key missing as well. On average <var>r</var> × <var>δ</var> requests arrive in that time, so:</p>
<div class="st-math">queries per expiry ≈ 1 + r × δ ≈ r × δ<br />trending: 500 requests a second × 0.2 s = 100 queries</div>
<p>All hundred return the same rows, so ninety-nine of them are wasted.</p>
<p>I checked the prediction against a real Redis 8.10.3. The application side was a small Node.js program with four connections standing in for four processes, sending 500 requests a second at random for a key that was not in Redis; the database was a 200 ms timer that counted its queries. Three runs sent 114, 115 and 92 queries for a key that needed one, around the 100 the formula predicts (the arrivals are random, and the Redis round trips add a little to <var>δ</var>).</p>
<p>The formula also says which keys cannot stampede. A key read twice a second with a 50 ms rebuild has <var>r</var> × <var>δ</var> = 0.1: most expiries see a single miss, and an occasional pair. Stampedes belong to keys that are read many times during one rebuild: the shared, expensive-to-build values like the home page panel, a configuration blob, a popular product, a leaderboard. Those are usually few, and you can name them.</p>
<p>To find them in a running system you need two numbers per key. Reads a second come from application metrics, or from sampling the keys your code reads. Rebuild time comes from timing the code that loads the key. The database gives a cross-check: the same expensive query arriving in bursts of identical copies, visible in its statement statistics, is a stampede. <ArticleRef slug="cluster-and-hot-keys">Redis Cluster, sharding and hot keys</ArticleRef> covers finding hot keys from the Redis side, and <ArticleRef slug="operating-a-cache">Operating a cache</ArticleRef> the metrics to watch.</p>
<p>An expiry is not the only way a copy disappears. A write that deletes the key starts the same stampede; I come back to that case once the defences are in place.</p>
</section>

<section id="database">
<h2>Why the database suffers more than the count suggests</h2>
<p>A hundred queries in 200 milliseconds might sound manageable. The trouble is what they do to the database's capacity. An application reaches its database through a connection pool of fixed size, and the database can only run so much at once. Figure 2 models a pool of 20 connections. Requests for the key arrive evenly from the moment it expires, each miss waits for a free connection, then holds it for the 200 ms query. One query for an unrelated page arrives 100 ms after the expiry and joins the same line.</p>
<PoolFigure number={2} />
<p>Notice the line marking where the new copy is stored. The first query finishes on time, at 200 ms, and from then on every request for the key is a hit. But the other 99 queries are already sent, and the database works through them 20 at a time for about another second, long after anyone needs their answers. The unrelated page waits about a third of a second for a connection. Slide the rate up to 1,000 a second: the duplicates keep the pool busy until about 2.2 seconds, and the other page waits about 0.9 seconds. Slide it down to 100: the 20 queries just fit the pool, and nothing else waits.</p>
<p>The arithmetic is short. <var>r</var> × <var>δ</var> queries, each taking <var>δ</var>, on <var>c</var> connections, keep the pool busy for about <var>r</var> × <var>δ</var> × <var>δ</var> / <var>c</var>: 100 × 0.2 / 20 = 1 second. The square of <var>δ</var> is the important part. A slower rebuild means both more duplicate queries and a longer time to run each one.</p>
<p>The model is kind to the database. It lets queries run without slowing one another, and nothing times out or retries. In a real database, a hundred copies of an aggregate compete for CPU and disk, so each takes longer than 200 ms. That lengthens <var>δ</var>, which lets more requests miss before the first copy is stored, which slows the queries further. Vattani and colleagues, whose early-refresh rule appears later in this article, note that stampedes are often called cascading failures for this reason. Timeouts and retries add load at the worst moment: a request that gives up after a second and tries again sends a second query for the same key. <ArticleRef slug="caching-patterns" hash="read-through">Caching patterns</ArticleRef> (Figure 4 there) showed where that leads when a whole cache is empty: a database that never catches up.</p>
</section>

<section id="single-flight">
<h2>Single-flight: one load per process</h2>
<p>The cheapest defence lives inside each application process. When a request misses, the process records that it is loading the key. When another request for the same key misses while that load is in flight, it does not query the database; it waits for the load already running and takes its result. When the load completes, every waiting request gets the same answer and the record is removed. This is called <strong>request coalescing</strong>, or single-flight after Go's <code>golang.org/x/sync/singleflight</code> package, which provides exactly this: <code>Do(key, fn)</code> runs <code>fn</code> once per key at a time and hands its result to every concurrent caller. Most languages have an equivalent, and in others it is a dictionary of in-flight futures keyed by the cache key.</p>
<p>Figure 3 adds single-flight to Figure 1. Requests waiting for their process's load are drawn as filled slots beside the process.</p>
<SingleFlightFigure number={3} />
<p>Each process now sends at most one query per expiry, so four processes send four. Switch to "every request queries" to compare. On a real Redis with the 500 requests a second from before, single-flight brought the 92 to 115 queries for the cold key down to exactly 4, one per process, in each of three runs. No request was slower than without it: the waiting requests were going to wait for a rebuild anyway, and now they wait for someone else's.</p>
<p>That is the attraction: no extra network round trips, no new failure modes in Redis, a few lines of code. Its limits come from its scope and from sharing:</p>
<ul>
<li>It works per process. Fifty application processes still send fifty queries. That may be good enough: fifty is far fewer than a few hundred, and it does not grow with traffic.</li>
<li>Everyone waiting shares the leader's fate. If the leader's query fails, every waiting request gets the error; if it hangs, they all hang. Give the load a timeout.</li>
<li>Every waiting request gets the same object. If code modifies what it receives, the others see the change. Treat shared results as read-only.</li>
</ul>
<p>To get from one query per process to one query in total, the processes have to agree on who rebuilds. They already share something they can agree through: Redis.</p>
</section>

<section id="lock">
<h2>A rebuild lock in Redis</h2>
<p>A <strong>rebuild lock</strong> is a second key that says "someone is rebuilding this one". The process that misses tries to create it. Redis's <code>SET</code> with <code>NX</code> ("only if the key does not exist") does that in one atomic step, so when several processes try at once, exactly one gets <code>OK</code> and the rest get nil:</p>
<pre><code>SET lock:trending 9f3c… NX PX 5000</code></pre>
<p>The value is a random token that only this process knows, and <code>PX 5000</code> gives the lock an expiry of 5 seconds, so a process that crashes while holding it does not block the key for ever. Figure 4 steps through a rebuild with two requests in different processes.</p>
<LockSequence number={4} />
<p>Two steps in it are easy to leave out, and both matter.</p>
<p>The first is reading the key again after taking the lock. Between A's miss and A's <code>SET … NX</code>, another process may have finished a rebuild, stored the copy and released its lock. Without a second read, A rebuilds a key that is already fresh. I measured this with 30 short bursts of 500 requests a second against a missing key: without the second read, 2 of the 30 bursts sent two queries instead of one; with it, none did.</p>
<p>The second is releasing only your own lock. A plain <code>DEL lock:trending</code> deletes whatever lock is there, including one that another process took after yours expired; the next section shows how that happens. The release should delete the key only if it still holds your token. Since Redis 8.4, <code>DELEX</code> does that in one command. It replies 1 if it deleted the key and 0 if the key held something else, which is what I saw on Redis 8.10.3:</p>
<pre><code>DELEX lock:trending IFEQ 9f3c…</code></pre>
<p>Valkey 9.0 and later have the same compare-and-delete as <code>DELIFEQ lock:trending 9f3c…</code>. On earlier versions of either, and on managed services that do not offer these commands yet, it is a short Lua script, which Redis runs without interleaving other commands.</p>
<pre><code>if redis.call("get", KEYS[1]) == ARGV[1] then
  return redis.call("del", KEYS[1])
else
  return 0
end</code></pre>
<p>Check which your server has: an unknown command raised inside the release code would hide whatever error the rebuild hit.</p>
<p>Put together in Python, the read path looks like this. The waiting requests poll the key; I come back to how long they should wait.</p>
<pre><code>{`def get_trending():
    cached = redis.get("trending")
    if cached is not None:
        return json.loads(cached)                       # a hit
    token = uuid4().hex
    if redis.set("lock:trending", token, nx=True, px=5_000):
        try:
            cached = redis.get("trending")              # filled while we were taking the lock?
            if cached is not None:
                return json.loads(cached)
            result = db.trending(timeout=2.0)           # the only query; the database stops it at 2 s
            redis.set("trending", json.dumps(result), ex=60)
            return result
        finally:
            # Release only our own lock (Redis 8.4 and later)
            redis.execute_command("DELEX", "lock:trending", "IFEQ", token)
    deadline = time.monotonic() + 1.0                   # somebody else is rebuilding
    while time.monotonic() < deadline:
        time.sleep(0.02)
        cached = redis.get("trending")
        if cached is not None:
            return json.loads(cached)
    raise CacheBusy("trending")                         # holder slow or gone`}</code></pre>
<p>Figure 5 runs the lock in the slowed-down model, and lets you switch back to single-flight.</p>
<LockFigure number={5} />
<p>Now each expiry sends exactly one query, however many processes there are. Notice what the readout for the slowest response does, though. With single-flight the slowest request waited about 1.1 seconds of this model's time, roughly one rebuild. With the lock it waits about 1.3 to 1.5 seconds: taking the lock costs a round trip to Redis, and a waiting request only learns that the copy is back at its next poll. The lock saves the database, not the readers. On the real Redis, the lock cut the cold key's queries to exactly 1 in each run, while 99 to 115 requests waited for it; the slowest request took 206 to 230 ms against 202 to 206 ms with single-flight, the difference being mostly the 20 ms polling interval.</p>
<p>The waiting bound and the query timeout need to agree. In the code above, waiters give up after 1 second but the holder's query may run for up to 2, so a rebuild that is slow but succeeds still fails every waiter in the gap. Either make the waiting bound cover the query timeout, or accept the gap and let the stale copy described in the next sections cover it. What waiting requests should do is a design choice, and each answer has a cost:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Poll and then give up</strong>, as above. Poll often enough that the extra wait is small next to the rebuild, and stop after a bound you would accept as a response time. The polling load is the number of waiters, about <var>r</var> × <var>δ</var>, times the polls each makes a second. For the trending key that is 100 waiters × 50 polls a second, 5,000 cheap <code>GET</code>s a second, which Redis handles easily. At 10,000 requests a second it would be 100,000 a second at one key, so put single-flight in front of the lock: then only one request per process polls. Add a little randomness to the interval so that the waiters do not poll in step.</li>
<li><strong>After giving up, go to the database anyway.</strong> This keeps the page working if the lock holder has died, but if the holder is merely slow because the database is overloaded, it sends the stampede the lock was meant to prevent. Prefer an error, a default, or the stale copy described in the next sections.</li>
<li><strong>Wait for a message instead of polling</strong>, for example the lock holder publishing on a channel when it stores the copy. It saves the polling, at the price of more moving parts. Polling is usually enough.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you read <ArticleRef slug="invalidation" hash="leases">Cache invalidation and consistency</ArticleRef>, this lock will look familiar. The lease there is the same primitive written into the key itself: a placeholder token stored with <code>SET … NX PX</code>, which also tells every other reader that someone is already loading the key. Facebook's memcache uses it in exactly this way, telling clients that find a lease to wait briefly and read again. If you already use leases, you have a rebuild lock: make the readers who find a placeholder wait and read again, instead of going to the database. If you do not, the separate lock key above leaves the cached value untouched, which makes it easier to combine with serving a stale copy.</p>
</section>

<section id="lock-hazards">
<h2>What can go wrong with the lock</h2>
<p>The lock has an expiry so that a crashed holder cannot block the key for ever. That same expiry is what lets it fail: Redis does not know whether the holder is still working, it only counts down. Figure 6 steps through a lock with a 300 ms expiry and a query that, on a bad day, takes 400 ms before it times out: a query timeout longer than the lock, which is exactly the configuration to avoid. Step through it with each way of releasing the lock.</p>
<HazardSequence number={6} />
<p>In both versions the lock expires while A is still working, and B takes it at 350 ms. Two rebuilds now run at once. That part is caused by the expiry being too short, and only a longer expiry, or extending the lock while the rebuild runs, fixes it. With a plain <code>DEL</code>, A's cleanup then deletes B's lock, and C takes a third. Each holder that fails and releases this way lets in another rebuilder, and failures cluster exactly when the database is slow. With <code>DELEX … IFEQ</code>, A's release finds B's token, deletes nothing and replies 0, and C waits for B. I reproduced both timelines on Redis 8.10.3 with real sleeps between the commands.</p>
<p>So choose the lock's expiry from the slowest rebuild you will tolerate, not the typical one, and make the rebuild give up before the lock expires. The timeout has to be one the database enforces, such as PostgreSQL's <code>statement_timeout</code> or MySQL's <code>MAX_EXECUTION_TIME</code> for reads. A timeout only in the client stops the application waiting, but leaves the query running in the database while the next rebuilder starts another. For the trending query, with a 200 ms typical time, a 2-second query timeout and a 5-second lock leave room. Some libraries also extend a lock while its holder works; that helps for long rebuilds, but the holder can still stall between extensions.</p>
<p>The expiry has a cost in the other direction too. If the holder crashes, the lock stays until it expires, and with the code above every reader of the key polls for a second and then fails. With a 5-second lock at 500 requests a second, that is up to about 2,500 failed page views. Three things shorten the gap: serve the stale copy, as the next section describes, so readers have something to show; keep the expiry no longer than the query timeout requires; and let waiters retry <code>SET … NX</code> as they poll, so one of them takes over as soon as the dead holder's lock expires.</p>
<p>Even a well-chosen expiry does not make the lock exact, and it does not need to be. The Redis documentation describes a lock on a single Redis instance as viable "where a race condition from time to time is acceptable". Three things can let two holders in:</p>
<ul>
<li>A holder that pauses past the expiry, in a long garbage collection or a stalled virtual machine, and then carries on as if it still held the lock.</li>
<li>A failover to a replica that had not yet received the lock, because Redis replication is asynchronous.</li>
<li>A change to the server's clock. As the same page notes, "Redis is not using monotonic clock for TTL expiration mechanism".</li>
</ul>
<p>For a rebuild lock each of these costs one extra query, which is harmless. Martin Kleppmann's distinction is the useful one: this is a lock for efficiency, to avoid doing the same work twice, not one for correctness. Never use it to guard something that must happen at most once, such as charging a card. If you need that, read the Redis distributed locks page and Kleppmann's article, linked at the end.</p>
</section>

<section id="stale">
<h2>Serving the stale copy while one request refreshes it</h2>
<p>The lock spares the database, but every request that arrives during the rebuild still waits for it. They wait because there is nothing to give them: the copy expired, so Redis deleted it. If the copy were still there, slightly past its intended life, most pages would rather show a list of trending products a few seconds old than make the visitor wait.</p>
<p>So keep it. Give the value two lifetimes: a <strong>soft expiry</strong>, after which it counts as stale and should be refreshed, and a <strong>hard expiry</strong>, after which it must not be served at all. Store it in Redis with the hard expiry as its TTL. On a read, fetch the value and its remaining lifetime together, either in a <code>MULTI</code> transaction or simply pipelined in one round trip:</p>
<pre><code>GET trending
PTTL trending</code></pre>
<p><code>PTTL</code> returns the remaining lifetime in milliseconds (−2 for a missing key, −1 for one without a TTL). If more than the hard-minus-soft margin remains, the copy is fresh. If less remains, it is stale: return it at once, and let one request, the one that takes the rebuild lock, refresh it in the background. That request reads <code>PTTL</code> again after taking the lock and refreshes only if the copy is still stale, for the same reason as the second read in the lock code: another request may have refreshed it and released the lock in the moment between the first read and the <code>SET … NX</code>. If the key is missing altogether, there is nothing to serve, and you are back to the lock and waiting. Figure 7 compares waiting for the lock holder with serving stale, with a soft expiry of 5 seconds and a hard expiry of 15.</p>
<StaleFigure number={7} />
<p>Notice the <Term kind="stale" mark>stale</Term> responses during each refresh, and the readout for the slowest response: with the lock it is about one and a half rebuilds, and with serving stale it stays at the time of an ordinary hit. Each expiry still sends one query. On the real Redis, over 30 seconds at 500 requests a second with the same soft and hard expiries, the lock sent 5 queries and made 535 requests wait, with the slowest taking 236 ms; serving stale sent the same 5 queries, served 523 requests a stale copy, and the slowest took 35 ms.</p>
<p>The re-check after taking the lock matters here. I ran serving stale eight times for 30 seconds each, with and without it. Without it, four of the eight runs sent 6 or 7 queries instead of 5: a second request had read the old copy just before the first refresh stored the new one, then took the lock the moment it was released and refreshed a copy that was already fresh. With the re-check, every run sent 5, and the re-check turned away 8 such refreshes.</p>
<p>The costs are modest but real. A visitor can see a value up to the soft expiry plus one rebuild old, longer if refreshes fail, up to the hard expiry. <ArticleRef slug="expiration-and-eviction">Expiration and eviction</ArticleRef> chose one TTL by weighing staleness against hit ratio; two expiries separate those decisions. Choose the soft expiry from the staleness you accept, and the hard expiry from how long a stale copy is better than none, for example to ride out a short database outage. Each key stays in memory for its hard expiry rather than its soft one. And a refresh that runs in the background needs somewhere to run and a way to report failures, since no request is waiting to see its error.</p>
<p>This idea is old. HTTP has it as the <code>stale-while-revalidate</code> cache directive (RFC 5861), which lets a cache keep serving a response for a stated number of seconds after it goes stale while it fetches a new one in the background. Facebook's memcache does the same after a delete: it keeps a recently deleted value for a short time and returns it, marked stale, to clients that can make progress with slightly old data, rather than making them wait for a lease holder.</p>
</section>

<section id="xfetch">
<h2>Refreshing early at random: XFetch</h2>
<p>Serving stale avoids waiting but still needs a lock to choose one refresher. Is there a way for requests to refresh a key before it expires, without coordinating at all? <ArticleRef slug="caching-patterns" hash="variations">Caching patterns</ArticleRef> described refresh-ahead: reload a key when its remaining lifetime falls below a threshold. Without coordination, though, every request that sees the key past the threshold reloads it, which is a stampede that starts earlier.</p>
<p>The fix, from a 2015 paper by Andrea Vattani, Flavio Chierichetti and Keegan Lowenstein, is to make the decision random, with the chance of refreshing rising as expiry approaches. Their algorithm, XFetch, gives each request a random head start. The request draws a time from an exponential distribution with an average of <var>β</var> × <var>δ</var>, where <var>δ</var> is the rebuild time and <var>β</var> a tuning constant, normally 1. If the request's arrival time plus its head start reaches the expiry, it refreshes; otherwise it uses the cached copy:</p>
<div class="st-math">head start = −δ × β × ln(random number between 0 and 1)<br />refresh if now + head start ≥ expiry</div>
<p>Far from the expiry, almost no head start is long enough. Close to it, many are. Figure 8 draws one expiry cycle at a time. Each request is a dot at the time it arrived, measured back from the expiry, with its head start as a line to its right. A line that reaches the dashed expiry line turns brown, and its request starts a rebuild, drawn as a numbered bar in the band below it, one lane per rebuild. The curve at the top is the chance that a request arriving at that moment refreshes. When the first rebuild stores the new copy, the figure holds the finished cycle for a moment before starting the next.</p>
<XFetchTimeline number={8} />
<p>Watch a few cycles. Most lines stop short, and the first one that reaches the expiry usually belongs to a request two or three seconds before it, which starts a rebuild that finishes well before the copy would have expired. Nobody misses. But the first rebuild is rarely the only one: while it runs, other requests keep drawing head starts, and some of them reach the expiry too. Those are duplicate rebuilds, numbered 2, 3 and so on in the band: a small stampede. Raise <var>β</var> and the curve widens: rebuilds start earlier and there are fewer duplicates. Lower it to 0.5 and they start later, there are more of them, and some cycles reach the expiry before the first rebuild finishes. The averages wander for a minute or two of simulated time; raise the speed to let them settle.</p>
<p>How many duplicates? The answer is surprising: about e<sup>1/β</sup> per expiry, the first rebuild included, which is 2.7 for <var>β</var> = 1, however busy the key is. The reasoning takes a few lines, if you want it.</p>
<p>Measure time <var>y</var> as seconds left before expiry. A request at <var>y</var> refreshes if its head start is at least <var>y</var>, which for an exponential head start with average <var>β</var><var>δ</var> happens with probability e<sup>−y/βδ</sup>. Requests arrive at <var>r</var> a second, so refreshes start at a rate that grows towards the expiry. Add that rate up from long before the expiry to the moment <var>y</var>, and you have the number of refreshes expected to have started by the time <var>y</var> seconds are left:</p>
<div class="st-math">refreshes started per second at y = r × e<sup>−y/βδ</sup><br />expected refreshes started by the time y seconds are left: H(y) = rβδ × e<sup>−y/βδ</sup></div>
<p>The first refresh comes roughly where <var>H</var> reaches 1, the point where one start is expected to have happened. More exactly, the value of <var>H</var> at the first start is random with an average of exactly 1, a general property of events that happen independently at random. That puts the first refresh about <var>β</var><var>δ</var>(ln(<var>r</var><var>β</var><var>δ</var>) + γ) seconds before the expiry, where γ ≈ 0.577 is the Euler–Mascheroni constant. The rebuild then runs for <var>δ</var> seconds, and the refreshes that start during it are as many as <var>H</var> grows over that window:</p>
<div class="st-math">extra refreshes = H(y − δ) − H(y) = H(y) × (e<sup>1/β</sup> − 1)<br />on average, with H(y) = 1 at the first start: e<sup>1/β</sup> − 1<br />refreshes per expiry, the first included = e<sup>1/β</sup> ≈ 2.7 for β = 1</div>
<p>The rate <var>r</var> has dropped out. The number of refreshes per expiry depends only on <var>β</var>: about 2.7 whether the key is read 100 or 10,000 times during a rebuild. Traffic only moves the first refresh earlier, and only slowly, with its logarithm. This assumes requests arrive independently of one another; bursty traffic gives somewhat more. Figure 9 simulates the rule many thousands of times so you can check both claims.</p>
<XFetchSize number={9} />
<p>Change the traffic and the simulated points stay on the curve; only the readout for the head start moves. Change <var>β</var> and you trade duplicates against earliness: <var>β</var> = 2 gives about 1.6 refreshes per expiry but starts them more than twice as early, which shortens the copy's useful life. Only with very little traffic, around 10 requests per rebuild time, and a small <var>β</var>, does the rule start too late to avoid misses.</p>
<p>The dotted line in Figure 9 is the paper's upper bound on the stampede size, e − 1/e ≈ 2.35 at <var>β</var> = 1 (the paper rounds to 2.4). It undercounts: simulating the paper's own model gives e<sup>1/β</sup>, above the bound at every <var>β</var>, and the paper's own experiment on a week of real traffic shows about 3 at <var>β</var> = 1. Plan for e<sup>1/β</sup>: at <var>β</var> = 1, about three rebuilds per expiry.</p>
<p>In Redis, XFetch needs the rebuild time stored with the value and the remaining lifetime read with it. A hash holds both, and a pipeline reads it with the TTL in one round trip:</p>
<pre><code>{`def get_trending(beta=1.0):
    pipe = redis.pipeline(transaction=False)
    pipe.hmget("trending", "v", "delta")
    pipe.pttl("trending")
    (value, delta_ms), ttl_ms = pipe.execute()
    if value is not None:                               # the write below always sets a TTL; PTTL −1 would refresh on every read
        head_start = -float(delta_ms) * beta * math.log(1.0 - random.random())
        if head_start < ttl_ms:
            return json.loads(value)                    # use the cached copy
    started = time.monotonic()
    result = db.trending()                              # refresh: early, or on a miss
    delta_ms = round((time.monotonic() - started) * 1000)
    pipe = redis.pipeline()                             # MULTI ... EXEC
    pipe.hset("trending", mapping={"v": json.dumps(result), "delta": delta_ms})
    pipe.pexpire("trending", 60_000)
    pipe.execute()
    return result`}</code></pre>
<p>I ran the same logic, written in JavaScript, against Redis 8.10.3 for 120 seconds at 500 requests a second, with the simulated 200 ms database and a 5-second TTL. The code works on Redis: the first rebuild started on average 1,028 and 1,036 ms before the expiry in two runs, against the 1,036 ms the formula predicts, and no request ever found the key missing. The rebuild counts (2.75 and 2.14 per expiry over about 28 expiries) are too noisy to pin down e, so the derivation and the simulation carry that claim.</p>
<p>XFetch's appeal is that it needs nothing beyond the value: no lock key, no polling, no background worker, and it works the same in every process. Its limits follow from the derivation. It cannot remove duplicates, only hold them to about e<sup>1/β</sup> per expiry, so with an expensive rebuild you may still want a lock behind it (the request that decides to refresh tries <code>SET … NX</code>, and if it loses, simply returns the current copy). It needs traffic: a key read less than about ten times per rebuild gets little warning, but such a key barely stampedes anyway. And the request that refreshes pays for it in latency, unless you hand the refresh to a background task and return the current copy.</p>
</section>

<section id="write-delete">
<h2>When a write deletes a hot key</h2>
<p>So far the copy disappeared because it expired. The usual way to keep a cache consistent removes it another way: a write to the database deletes the cached key, and the next read fills it again. For a hot key that delete is an expiry at a moment you did not choose, and the same <var>r</var> × <var>δ</var> requests miss behind it. A key that is written often stampedes after every write. This is the case Facebook's memcache paper calls a thundering herd: a key that is both read heavily and written often.</p>
<p>Not every defence carries over:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Single-flight and the rebuild lock work unchanged.</strong> They act on the miss, whatever caused it.</li>
<li><strong>Serving stale has nothing to serve.</strong> The delete removed the copy, and the stale copy with it.</li>
<li><strong>XFetch cannot help.</strong> It refreshes ahead of an expiry it can see coming; a write gives no warning.</li>
</ul>
<p>If readers can accept a slightly old value for a moment, the write can mark the copy stale instead of deleting it. With the soft and hard expiries above, that is one command, for example <code>PEXPIRE trending 5000 LT</code>. The <code>LT</code> option (Redis 7.0 and later) only ever shortens the lifetime, so a copy with less than 5 seconds left is not given more. Any remaining lifetime below the hard-minus-soft margin counts as stale, so readers keep receiving the old copy while one of them refreshes it behind the lock, and they can receive it for at most those 5 seconds if the refresh fails. It is what Facebook's memcache achieves by keeping deleted values for a short time, as the stale section mentioned. Whether a copy that is briefly stale after a write is acceptable, and the races a write can have with a refill already in flight, are questions about consistency rather than load; <ArticleRef slug="invalidation">Cache invalidation and consistency</ArticleRef> works through them, including what goes wrong when writers update the copy instead of deleting it.</p>
</section>

<section id="many-keys">
<h2>Many keys expiring together is a different problem</h2>
<p>A related pattern also produces a database spike: thousands of keys filled at the same moment, by a deploy or a batch job, all expiring at the same moment. Each key may have only one miss, but they all arrive together. <ArticleRef slug="expiration-and-eviction" hash="jitter">Expiration and eviction</ArticleRef> covered it, with a figure, and its remedy: jitter, a small random spread added to each key's TTL so the expiries drift apart.</p>
<p>The two problems need different remedies, so it is worth telling them apart. Jitter cannot help a single hot key: however its TTL is chosen, it expires at some moment, and all its readers are waiting at that moment. And the defences in this article do nothing for many cold keys expiring together, because each of those keys is missed once. When the spike comes from one key, or a handful, that are read many times during a rebuild, coalesce the rebuilds or refresh early. When it comes from many keys that were filled together, jitter their TTLs.</p>
</section>

<section id="cold-start">
<h2>A cold start stampedes every key at once</h2>
<p>So far, either one key stampeded or many keys missed once each. When Redis restarts empty, or a new cache cluster comes up, or someone flushes it, every key is missing at the same moment, and every popular key stampedes at once. Now the duplicates compete not with one unrelated page but with the misses for every other key, at the moment the database has the most work it will ever get.</p>
<p>Figure 10 runs the scenario from <ArticleRef slug="caching-patterns" hash="read-through">Caching patterns</ArticleRef> (Figure 4 there): 500 requests a second over 1,000 keys, where a few keys are very popular and most are rarely read, a database that answers queries one after another, and requests that give up after one second. As that article recommended, a fill that arrives after its request has given up is still stored, so the cache always recovers. The two runs differ only in whether a miss for a key that is already being loaded sends another query (no coalescing), or waits for that load, as a per-key lock or single-flight would make it.</p>
<ColdStartFigure number={10} />
<p>Start with the upper chart, the queue for the database. With the database answering 100 queries a second and no coalescing, the queue peaks at more than four seconds, against one and a half with one query per key. A request gives up after one second, and the queue stays above a second for about 12 seconds, so for that long nearly every miss that sends a query fails before its answer arrives; with one query per key, it is about 4 seconds. More than a third of the queries sent without coalescing are duplicates of a load already under way, for the most popular keys, the ones that matter most. They hold up the loads of keys nobody has asked for yet, so the hit ratio in the lower chart takes about 10 seconds to reach 90%, against about 7 seconds with one query per key. Slide the capacity down to 60 and the gap widens to about 21 seconds against 10. Slide it up to 150 or more and the two runs look alike: when the database can keep up, the duplicates are a waste but not a danger.</p>
<p>Two of the defences do nothing here. There is no stale copy to serve, and XFetch acts only on keys that have an expiry to refresh ahead of. Coalescing, per process or with a lock, is what helps. The rest of the answer is to avoid a fully cold start: fill the most popular keys before sending traffic, restart cache nodes one at a time, and shed load while the cache refills. Those are operational procedures, and <ArticleRef slug="operating-a-cache">Operating a cache</ArticleRef> covers them.</p>
</section>

<section id="choosing">
<h2>Choosing a defence</h2>
<p>The defences combine, and most production systems with a genuinely hot key use two of them. This table puts them side by side. The last column but one matters when the key is deleted on write: only the defences that act on a miss help then.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Stampede defences, what they stop and what they cost">
<table>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Defence</th><th scope="col">What it stops</th><th scope="col">What it costs</th><th scope="col">After a delete on write</th><th scope="col">Redis commands</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">TTL jitter</th><td>Many keys filled together expiring together</td><td>Nothing, if the longest TTL is tolerable</td><td>Not relevant</td><td>A random <code>EX</code></td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Single-flight</th><td>Duplicate queries within a process; one per process remains</td><td>A shared result and a shared failure; no extra round trips</td><td>Works</td><td>None</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Rebuild lock</th><td>All duplicate queries for a missing key</td><td>Waiting requests and polling; a lock expiry to choose; not exact</td><td>Works</td><td><code>SET … NX PX</code>, <code>DELEX … IFEQ</code> (8.4+) or a script</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Serve stale</th><td>Waiting during a refresh</td><td>Staleness up to the soft expiry plus a rebuild; memory for the hard expiry; still needs a lock to refresh once</td><td>Nothing to serve, unless the write marks the copy stale instead</td><td><code>GET</code> and <code>PTTL</code> pipelined, plus the lock</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">XFetch</th><td>Misses on a hot key; most duplicates, with no coordination</td><td>About e<sup>1/β</sup> rebuilds per expiry; a rebuild time stored with each value</td><td>No help</td><td><code>HMGET</code> and <code>PTTL</code> pipelined</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Background refresh</th><td>Every miss on a known key</td><td>A worker to run and monitor; refreshes keys nobody reads; still needs a protected miss path</td><td>No help, unless the write triggers the refresh</td><td>A scheduled <code>SET … EX</code></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>Four questions settle it for a given key.</p>
<p><strong>Can it stampede?</strong> Multiply its reads a second by its rebuild time. Below 1, a stampede is a couple of queries; do nothing beyond jitter. Well above 1, read on.</p>
<p><strong>How bad is a duplicate rebuild?</strong> If the database can absorb a few copies of the query, one per process or about three per expiry, single-flight or XFetch alone keeps the number that small and costs least. If even a few copies hurt, use a rebuild lock, so that one query runs however many processes there are.</p>
<p><strong>Can readers accept a slightly old value?</strong> If yes, serve stale with a background refresh behind the lock, and no reader ever waits for a hot key except on a cold start; if writes delete the key, have them mark it stale instead. If no, readers must wait for the rebuild, and XFetch is attractive because it starts the rebuild before anyone needs to wait, though not after a write.</p>
<p><strong>Is the key known in advance and always read?</strong> The home page panel is. For a handful of such keys, a background job that refreshes them on a schedule removes the expiry stampede. Readers still need a protected miss path, a lock or single-flight, for when the key is evicted, flushed or lost in a restart, or the job fails. The paper's authors note the main reason background refresh is not the default: one more process to run and watch, and work spent on keys that may no longer be read.</p>
<p>For the trending panel, a reasonable answer is to serve stale with a soft expiry of 60 seconds and a hard expiry of 10 minutes, refresh behind a rebuild lock with a 5-second expiry and a 2-second query timeout. When the key is missing altogether, after a restart, requests wait on the same lock, so the database still sees one query; single-flight in each process would then keep the waiting requests from all polling Redis.</p>
</section>

<section id="decide">
<h2>What you can now decide</h2>
<p>You can predict a stampede's size as reads a second times rebuild time, and the time it keeps a pool busy as that number times the rebuild time over the pool size, and so find the few keys that can stampede at all. You can tell a hot key's stampede from many keys expiring together, and use jitter only for the second. You can coalesce rebuilds within a process, implement a rebuild lock with <code>SET … NX PX</code>, a second read and a token-checked release, choose its expiry from the slowest rebuild, and know why it is an efficiency lock that may occasionally let two rebuilders in. You can keep a stale copy with soft and hard expiries so readers do not wait while a copy exists, refresh early with XFetch and expect about e<sup>1/β</sup> rebuilds per expiry, and you know that a write that deletes the key, or a cold start, defeats both: a delete needs the lock or a write that marks the copy stale, and a cold start needs coalescing and an operational plan.</p>
<p>All of these defences add commands to the read path: a <code>PTTL</code> here, a <code>SET … NX</code> there, polling loops. Whether those are cheap depends on how Redis executes commands, which <ArticleRef slug="how-redis-works">How Redis works</ArticleRef> explains, and on round trips and atomicity, the subject of <ArticleRef slug="clients-and-pipelining">Redis clients: round trips, pipelining and atomic operations</ArticleRef>. A key so hot that even its hits overload one Redis server is a different problem from a stampede, covered in <ArticleRef slug="cluster-and-hot-keys">Redis Cluster, sharding and hot keys</ArticleRef>, and warming a cold cache is part of <ArticleRef slug="operating-a-cache">Operating a cache</ArticleRef>.</p>
</section>

